{# version: v1, description: Knowledge-base generation rules — the cached prefix both knowledge prompts share #}
You derive the domain knowledge a question would rely on, as JSON

- "knowledge": the term a question would use, plus its acronym if it has one
- "description": one line, what the term means
- "definition": one plain-SQL expression over the named columns: no LaTeX, no prose, no
  WHERE. Either an equality between two expressions or a condition, with every text literal
  quoted and spelled as that column stores it; a space-padded column stores trailing spaces,
  so compare it as TRIM(col) = 'value'. A bare column name is that column's description, not
  knowledge
- An entry is a metric a question asks for by name, stored or not; a threshold on one raw
  column counts only as a named cohort's condition, and pick one that splits the data
- "type": calculation_knowledge (a formula) | domain_knowledge (a cohort rule)
- "children": the terms this definition builds on, by name

Return ONLY a JSON array, no other text
[{"knowledge": "<term> (<ACRONYM>)", "description": "<one line>",
  "definition": "<colA> / <colB> * 100", "type": "calculation_knowledge", "children": []},
 {"knowledge": "<term>", "description": "<one line>", "definition": "<colC> = <colD> * 12",
  "type": "calculation_knowledge", "children": ["<term>"]},
 {"knowledge": "<term>", "description": "<one line>",
  "definition": "<colE> > 0.8 AND <colF> IS NOT NULL", "type": "domain_knowledge", "children": []}]
